1
00:00:00,301 --> 00:00:06,054
This is a DevOps on-call assistant built with Google's Agent Development Kit and Gemini on Vertex AI.

2
00:00:06,054 --> 00:00:15,794
It answers operational questions from our runbooks using retrieval-augmented generation, and it can act: opening incident tickets in Firestore and checking service health.

3
00:00:15,794 --> 00:00:22,117
It runs on Cloud Run as a dedicated service account with no keys, and everything is provisioned with Terraform.

4
00:00:23,301 --> 00:00:25,080
First, the security posture.

5
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:32,705
The Cloud Run service is not public: an unauthenticated request gets a 403, and the IAM policy has no all-users binding.

6
00:00:32,705 --> 00:00:42,935
The runtime identity is a dedicated service account with just two project roles, Vertex AI user and Datastore user, plus read-only access on the runbooks bucket.

7
00:00:42,935 --> 00:00:45,667
There are no service account keys anywhere.

8
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:51,957
Locally I reach the service through gcloud run services proxy, which injects my own identity token.

9
00:00:53,331 --> 00:00:56,849
Now the agent itself, in the ADK web UI served by Cloud Run.

10
00:00:56,849 --> 00:00:59,371
I ask how to recover CouchDB after a crash.

11
00:00:59,371 --> 00:01:07,228
The agent calls search runbooks, which embeds the question with Vertex AI and runs a cosine similarity search over the runbook chunks.

12
00:01:07,228 --> 00:01:13,854
It then reads the best-matching runbook in full and answers with the exact ordered steps, citing the source file.

13
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:20,715
It is instructed to answer only from the runbooks, so it will say so when something is not covered rather than guess.

14
00:01:26,885 --> 00:01:36,201
The left panel shows the event and trace view: the model's function call to search runbooks, the tool result, the read runbook call, and the final response.

15
00:01:36,201 --> 00:01:42,053
This is the same observability you get from ADK locally, now running against the deployed service.

16
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:44,246
Next, an action.

17
00:01:44,246 --> 00:01:47,693
I ask the agent to open a P1 ticket for this incident.

18
00:01:47,693 --> 00:02:01,927
Gemini fills the tool arguments from the conversation, a title, the severity, and a summary, and calls create incident ticket, which writes a document to Firestore using the service account, with no credentials in the code.

19
00:02:01,927 --> 00:02:04,033
The agent confirms the ticket id.

20
00:02:06,394 --> 00:02:08,539
The agent can chain tools in one turn.

21
00:02:08,539 --> 00:02:19,378
Here it checks the queue processor through the status tool, and then lists the open tickets from Firestore, newest first, using a composite index that Terraform created alongside the database.

22
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,496
Let's verify that write in the Firestore console.

23
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:39,046
The tickets collection shows the new document with the fields the agent wrote: title, severity, summary, status and a server timestamp.

24
00:02:40,921 --> 00:02:44,977
And finally the Cloud Run service itself, on its security tab.

25
00:02:46,152 --> 00:02:53,928
It confirms what we saw on the command line: authentication is required, and the service identity is the dedicated sa ops agent service account.

26
00:02:53,928 --> 00:02:55,440
No keys were created for it.

27
00:02:57,308 --> 00:03:10,169
Everything you saw is defined in Terraform: APIs, the service account and its roles, the private bucket with the runbooks, Firestore and its index, Artifact Registry and the Cloud Run service.

28
00:03:10,169 --> 00:03:15,327
The image is built by Cloud Build with its own least-privilege build account.

29
00:03:15,327 --> 00:03:21,624
The code, Terraform and this recording are in the GitHub repository linked in the description.

30
00:03:21,624 --> 00:03:22,964
Thanks for watching.
